WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:06.189
When it comes to global box office, China
is projected to overtake the United States

00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:09.570
as the biggest market in the world by 2020.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:14.849
Chinese law, however, only allows 34 Hollywood
films to be released on the mainland each

00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:20.010
year, meaning there's an intense competition
to earn one of those coveted slots.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:25.190
And since Chinese censors are notoriously
strict, outright banning movies they don't

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:30.010
like, Hollywood studios are more than happy
to make changes to their films in order to

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:31.770
get a piece of the action.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:37.780
Here's a look at some movies Hollywood was
forced to change for China's sake.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.210
Doctor Strange

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.310
Whitewashing is nothing new in Hollywood,
but the problem has come to a head in recent

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:46.970
years with a bunch of controversial against-race
castings.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:52.760
In 2016, Marvel Studios came under fire for
Doctor Strange, which cast Tilda Swinton in

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:57.010
the role of The Ancient One, who, in the comics,
was a Tibetan mystic.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:02.680
According to the film's co-writer, C. Robert
Cargill, that presented Marvel with a major

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:08.499
problem, because the Chinese government refuses
to recognize Tibet as an independent country.

00:01:08.499 --> 00:01:13.389
He told the Double Toasted podcast, "The Ancient
One was a racist stereotype who comes from

00:01:13.389 --> 00:01:16.799
a region of the world that is in a very weird
political place.

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:18.439
He originates from Tibet.

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:23.299
So if you acknowledge that Tibet is a place
and that he's Tibetan, you risk alienating

00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:28.450
one billion people [...] and risk the Chinese
government going, 'Hey, you know one of the

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:30.179
biggest film-watching countries in the world?

00:01:30.179 --> 00:01:34.090
We're not going to show your movie because
you decided to get political.'"

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:35.090
Skyfall

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:41.179
2006's Casino Royale made history as, after
20 previous attempts to get him there had

00:01:41.179 --> 00:01:45.909
proven unsuccessful, James Bond was finally
introduced to China.

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:52.560
And the cost was just a few new lines of dialogue
from Dame Judi Dench glossing over that whole

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:53.990
Cold War thing.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:57.729
"In the old days if an agent did something
that embarrassing he'd have the good sense

00:01:57.729 --> 00:01:58.729
to defect.

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:01.249
Christ, I miss the Cold War."

00:02:01.249 --> 00:02:06.479
However, 2012's Skyfall was subjected to heavy
editing at the hands of Chinese censors, who

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:11.090
removed all references to torture tactics
used by the Chinese authorities as well as

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:16.200
cutting a scene in which a Chinese security
guard is killed during an assassination.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:22.040
They decided to leave in a scene in which
Bond asks a Chinese casino hostess about child

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:27.150
prostitution, though the Chinese subtitles
deliberately misquote the secret agent, changing

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:31.690
his question to one about connections to the
mafia as opposed to trafficking.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:35.439
"Tattoo on your wrist is Macau sex trade.

00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:36.930
You belong to one of the houses.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.140
What were you, 12?"

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:40.970
Iron Man 3

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:46.599
One of the most shameless attempts at a China
cash grab in recent years, Marvel's Iron Man

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:51.790
3 was influenced by Hollywood's newest eastern
audience in more ways than one — the first

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.069
being the new take on Tony Stark's arch-nemesis.

00:02:55.069 --> 00:03:00.609
There was clearly no way the racially insensitive
Mandarin would ever be allowed to appear on

00:03:00.609 --> 00:03:03.859
the big screen in China in his original comic
book form.

00:03:03.859 --> 00:03:08.440
So Marvel Studios and Iron Man 3 director
Shane Black decided to scrap that version

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:14.359
entirely in favor of an all new, non-Asian,
fake Mandarin played by Sir Ben Kingsley.

00:03:14.359 --> 00:03:15.829
"Don't hurt the face!

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:17.359
I'm an actor."

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:18.500
"You've got a minute to live.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:19.240
Fill it with words."

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.460
"Just a role.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:22.460
The Mandarin!

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:23.620
See, it's not real."

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.980
But that was just the tip of the iceberg.

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:30.450
The studio also filmed a number of scenes
specifically for the Chinese release, cutting

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:35.280
in four extra minutes of footage filled with
product placement and a new Chinese mentor

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:41.819
for Tony Stark, Dr. Wu, who performs surgery
on Stark and has a conversation with his computerized

00:03:41.819 --> 00:03:43.780
assistant, J.A.R.V.I.S., in Mandarin.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:48.120
The move backfired, however, with Chinese
viewers seeing through the product placement

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.129
and questioning the logic of Dr. Wu's relevance
to the story.

00:03:52.129 --> 00:03:54.819
Since, y'know, he had none.

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:56.859
Rogue One: A Star Wars Story

00:03:56.859 --> 00:04:01.140
Hollywood movies weren't shown in China at
all when the original Star Wars trilogy made

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:06.569
waves domestically, and as a result, George
Lucas' space opera just isn't very well known

00:04:06.569 --> 00:04:07.569
there.

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:11.859
That fact showed plainly in the meager box
office takings for The Force Awakens.

00:04:11.859 --> 00:04:17.350
Episode VII's poor reception was clearly a
cause for concern at Disney, who made moves

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:23.400
to ensure that 2016's Rogue One: A Star Wars
Story didn't suffer the same fate.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.190
Chinese stars Jiang Wen and Donnie Yen were
hired in genuine supporting roles in an effort

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:32.440
to drum up interest in the spinoff, and their
inclusion created plenty of hype.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:37.790
Hong Kong-born Yen, China's second biggest
action star after Jackie Chan, initially refused

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:42.450
the role of Chirrut over fears that his participation
was just a marketing ploy.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:46.200
However, director Gareth Edwards managed to
convince him otherwise.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:51.390
Yen told the South China Morning Post, "When
the director approached me, I began by asking,

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:55.850
quite frankly, if it's a real character, or
if he's only casting me based on commercial

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.470
considerations for the [Chinese] market.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:03.560
Once he told me what my character was going
to be like, and some of my lines, including

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:08.440
the iconic 'May the Force be with you,' I
recognized the importance of my character."

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:12.790
Unfortunately, despite Edwards sticking to
his word and even allowing Yen to choreograph

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:18.260
his own action sequences, Chinese audiences
still didn't buy it, and the film bombed there.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:23.160
Jonathan Papish from China Film Insider told
TheWrap that "the small number of Donnie Yen

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:28.500
or Jiang Wen fans who turned out to see their
faces were completely confused or bored with

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.490
the rest of the story."

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:32.910
Pirates of the Caribbean: At World's End

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:36.970
Disney responded to reports that Pirates of
the Caribbean: At World's End, had suffered

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:41.930
quite the cull at the hands of China's censors
by admitting that there had been some cuts.

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:45.910
But the Mouse House declined to elaborate
further.

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:50.620
The franchise's previous installment, Dead
Man's Chest, didn't get a release at all because

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:52.520
of its ghostly elements.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:57.300
So for the series' third movie, Disney was
happy to part ways with a number of scenes

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.820
in order to push it through.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:04.960
Surprisingly, though, the scenes Chinese censors
asked to be cut all involved Chow Yun-fat,

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:08.320
one of the most famous action stars in Chinese
cinema.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:12.860
It seems strange that Chinese authorities
would insist on scenes featuring one of their

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:17.810
homegrown stars being removed from the film,
but they were reportedly very unhappy with

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.190
Yun-fat's character Sao Feng.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:25.140
The pirate lord of the South China Sea appears
in traditional Qing dynasty garb with a shiny

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:30.090
bald head and long, dirty fingernails, an
image censors in the country found to be an

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:31.930
offensive stereotype.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:35.680
Most of the scenes setting up the character
were deleted, making his sudden appearance

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:39.360
and death very confusing to Chinese audiences.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.880
The Hunger Games: Mockingjay - Part 1

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:46.940
China insists that 14 of the 34 Hollywood
films it approves for mainland screening each

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:53.520
year must be presented in either 3D or IMAX,
formats that have proven incredibly popular

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:56.410
there after heavy investment in the technology.

00:06:56.410 --> 00:07:01.300
Lionsgate used this to their advantage when
it came to releasing The Hunger Games: Mockingjay

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:06.610
– Part 1 in China, hoping to improve upon
the disappointing returns of previous installment,

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:07.610
Catching Fire.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:11.521
The first part of the franchise finale was
converted to 3D especially for China, and

00:07:11.521 --> 00:07:16.230
when it easily bested the previous films at
the box office, Part 2 was given the same

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:17.230
treatment.

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:21.680
Converting movies to 3D to make a few extra
bucks doesn't always work out, however.

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.240
The Bourne movies are very well known for
employing an excess of shaky-cam in their

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:31.970
nauseatingly shot action sequences, yet Universal
sought to milk fifth installment Jason Bourne

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:35.170
for all it was worth and released a 3D version
in China.

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:39.540
According to Quartz, the result was "a line
of people throwing up in the restroom of the

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:44.660
cinema," with one film fan begging Hollywood
not to do anything that dumb again.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:48.130
"Please don't offer 3D just to grab money
anymore.

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:52.620
Chinese people are not stupid, we can vote
with our feet and money."

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:53.700
Pixels

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:58.690
When Sony was subject to a cyber attack from
what many suspect were hackers acting on behalf

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:03.170
of the North Korean government, a number of
sensitive emails were dumped onto the internet

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:04.620
for all to see.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:09.900
The leak revealed details of how the Adam
Sandler comedy Pixels was changed for China:

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:14.380
a line of dialogue mentioning a "Communist
conspiracy" was given the axe, and a scene

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:18.980
in which the country's beloved landmark the
Great Wall comes under fire by the film's

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:23.250
arcade-themed alien invaders was cut from
the script before filming began.

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:28.920
Li Chow, Sony's chief rep in China, told his
stateside executives that "even though breaking

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:33.700
a hole on the Great Wall may not be a problem
as long as it is part of a worldwide phenomenon,

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:38.070
it is actually unnecessary because it will
not benefit the China release at all.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:41.000
I would then, recommend not to do it."

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.580
Chow's recommendations didn't make a whole
lot of difference in the end as the much-maligned

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:50.140
sci-fi comedy only pulled in $15 million there
anyway.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:51.140
Red Dawn

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:56.880
At one stage it looked as though the planned
remake of 1984 Cold War cult classic Red Dawn

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.610
was going to be shelved when its original
studio, MGM, ran into financial difficulties.

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:05.970
Contrafilm took the reins and the film finally
got the green light for 2012, though even

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:09.690
after the production wrapped, the problems
were far from over.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:14.070
Chinese news outlets learned that the Soviet
army that waged war on small-town America

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:18.500
in the original Red Dawn had been replaced
by Chinese invaders.

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:24.180
The result came in the form of headlines,
like "US re-shoots Cold War Movie to Demonize

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:25.180
China."

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:28.790
Naturally, the studio's execs panicked about
their chances in the Asian market.

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:33.240
A decision was made to change the invading
Chinese army to a North Korean one, with five

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:38.090
different special effects companies brought
in to restore hopes of scoring big in China.

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:43.500
They were given the unenviable task of removing
all references to a country that was essential

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:48.320
to the plot, digitally altering dialogue,
and replacing every Chinese flag and symbol.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:53.160
Dan Mintz from Chinese-based distributor DMG
Entertainment said that if Red Dawn had been

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:57.260
released in its original form in China, "there
would have been a real backlash.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:01.640
It's like being invited to a dinner party
and insulting the host all night long."

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:03.270
World War Z

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:07.890
Considering that World War Z spent years in
development hell, with Paramount completely

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:13.521
rewriting and reshooting the last 40 minutes
of the film at a cost of $75 million, you

00:10:13.521 --> 00:10:17.700
wouldn't think the studio would have time
to worry about trivial things like where the

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:19.630
offending virus originated.

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:23.930
As you've probably guessed by now, though,
the zombie outbreak that ultimately consumes

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:27.930
the world in World War Z was supposed to start
in China.

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:33.870
Instead, producers changed that to Hollywood's
new favorite pariah — North Korea — minimizing

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.920
the risk of getting on the wrong side of the
Chinese censors.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:39.120
"Why'd you sell guns to the North?"

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:39.980
"Why not?"

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:42.360
"Are they surviving this?"

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:43.000
"Indeed they are."

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:47.520
In the end their re-shoots and nitpicking
paid off in a big way, as Paramount was able

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:52.320
to boast that World War Z ended up becoming
Brad Pitt's highest-grossing film to date.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:58.560
While it failed to recoup its budget domestically,
it earned more than $540 million after worldwide

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.110
receipts were tallied.

00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:01.110
Thanks for watching!

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:03.840
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.439
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

